WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.859
Welcome to Module 3 of Global Secure
Access as the Identity-Aware Network:

2
00:00:04.859 --> 00:00:08.013
the compliant network
and tenant restrictions.

3
00:00:08.013 --> 00:00:12.442
These are the two signals only Microsoft
Entra can produce from the network,

4
00:00:12.442 --> 00:00:16.662
and both come with the Microsoft
traffic profile you enabled in Module 2.

5
00:00:16.662 --> 00:00:21.204
Both can lock people out, so this module
teaches their failure modes first,

6
00:00:21.204 --> 00:00:25.075
and runs them in report-only or
a pilot before they block anyone.

7
00:00:25.075 --> 00:00:30.028
It covers Steps 7 and 8 of the Global
Secure Access implementation guide,

8
00:00:30.028 --> 00:00:35.000
and takes about forty-five minutes,
plus a lab in your own test tenant.

9
00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:40.100
Why do these two matter?

10
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:45.019
The compliant network check binds sign-in
and token use to the agency's own path,

11
00:00:45.019 --> 00:00:48.575
so a token stolen and replayed
from somewhere else fails.

12
00:00:48.575 --> 00:00:53.025
In attack terms, it interrupts
credential access and defense evasion.

13
00:00:53.025 --> 00:00:57.088
Universal tenant restrictions
keep agency devices from signing

14
00:00:57.088 --> 00:01:01.150
in to a personal or hostile tenant,
which interrupts exfiltration.

15
00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:04.075
Both are powerful, and
both can lock people out.

16
00:01:04.075 --> 00:01:07.347
So in this module you learn
their failure modes first,

17
00:01:07.347 --> 00:01:11.640
and you run each in report-only or
a pilot before it blocks anyone.

18
00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:18.303
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

19
00:01:18.303 --> 00:01:22.526
You'll explain what the compliant
network check denies at authentication,

20
00:01:22.526 --> 00:01:24.502
and what it denies in near real time.

21
00:01:24.502 --> 00:01:27.243
You'll create the pilot
policy in report-only,

22
00:01:27.243 --> 00:01:31.614
with the emergency access group and
the Intune applications excluded.

23
00:01:31.614 --> 00:01:35.700
You'll list the five failure modes
and the design response to each,

24
00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.440
including a service outage.

25
00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.979
You'll turn on universal tenant
restrictions after the tenant

26
00:01:40.979 --> 00:01:44.927
restrictions version two policy, and
say which data planes it reaches.

27
00:01:44.927 --> 00:01:48.340
And you'll run Learn's four-case
test and read the block log.

28
00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:52.979
You need Module 2, with the Microsoft
traffic profile and signaling on,

29
00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:57.778
and Access and Privilege, appendix C,
for the tenant restrictions policy.

30
00:01:57.778 --> 00:02:01.290
The lab runs in a test
tenant, never production.

31
00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.553
Unit 1 is the compliant network check.

32
00:02:07.553 --> 00:02:12.323
It passes only for users connected through
this tenant's Global Secure Access,

33
00:02:12.323 --> 00:02:14.452
from the client or a remote network.

34
00:02:14.452 --> 00:02:18.216
At authentication, it denies
a replayed refresh token.

35
00:02:18.216 --> 00:02:22.318
And with services that support
Continuous Access Evaluation,

36
00:02:22.318 --> 00:02:27.290
such as Microsoft Graph, it rejects a
replayed access token in near real time.

37
00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:32.753
Turning signaling on creates a named
location, All Compliant Network locations.

38
00:02:32.753 --> 00:02:35.460
The pilot policy blocks all resources,

39
00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:39.352
for the pilot group, from
every location except that one.

40
00:02:39.352 --> 00:02:44.476
It excludes the emergency access group,
and, following Learn, Microsoft Intune

41
00:02:44.476 --> 00:02:46.683
Enrollment and Microsoft Intune,

42
00:02:46.683 --> 00:02:50.152
to avoid a circular dependency
where devices enroll.

43
00:02:50.152 --> 00:02:53.078
Learn's example sets the
policy straight to on.

44
00:02:53.078 --> 00:02:59.240
The guide, like every library policy, starts
in report-only, for ten business days.

45
00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:07.178
Unit 2 is the failure modes, which
you learn before you enforce.

46
00:03:07.178 --> 00:03:11.384
A disabled or missing client is
blocked at the next authentication;

47
00:03:11.384 --> 00:03:14.765
existing sessions continue
until reauthentication.

48
00:03:14.765 --> 00:03:19.603
The response is to restrict disabling and
give the help desk a reinstall script.

49
00:03:19.603 --> 00:03:21.770
In a Global Secure Access outage,

50
00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:25.140
users can't reach the
resources the check protects.

51
00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:29.271
Microsoft's break-glass scripts
switch these policies to report-only,

52
00:03:29.271 --> 00:03:33.402
and the guide rehearses them and
records them in the contingency plan.

53
00:03:33.402 --> 00:03:37.117
Turning signaling off while
policies are on blocks users,

54
00:03:37.117 --> 00:03:40.677
so the rollback order is
policies first, signaling last.

55
00:03:40.677 --> 00:03:43.665
The check isn't supported
for Private Access apps,

56
00:03:43.665 --> 00:03:46.652
which get the per-app
policies of Module 4 instead.

57
00:03:46.652 --> 00:03:51.389
And mobile devices need Defender
exclusions, outside this Windows pilot.

58
00:03:51.389 --> 00:03:55.452
One more piece: Universal
Continuous Access Evaluation.

59
00:03:55.452 --> 00:03:59.621
The Windows client one point
eight point two thirty-nine or

60
00:03:59.621 --> 00:04:03.703
later uses Universal C A E
tokens, revoked in near real time.

61
00:04:03.703 --> 00:04:07.969
Learn's recommendation is not to
disable C A E for these workloads,

62
00:04:07.969 --> 00:04:13.460
and the guide decides strict location
enforcement per policy, not tenant-wide.

63
00:04:17.210 --> 00:04:20.223
Unit 3 is universal tenant restrictions.

64
00:04:20.223 --> 00:04:24.846
The tenant restrictions version two
policy comes first: default block,

65
00:04:24.846 --> 00:04:30.254
with partner policies for the agency's own
other tenants and each approved partner,

66
00:04:30.254 --> 00:04:32.997
as Access and Privilege,
appendix C, sets it.

67
00:04:32.997 --> 00:04:38.219
Then tagging applies that policy to devices
with the client, or on a remote network,

68
00:04:38.219 --> 00:04:40.753
without a proxy, so users can sign in only

69
00:04:40.753 --> 00:04:43.748
to tenants and applications
the policy allows.

70
00:04:43.748 --> 00:04:48.697
Know its reach: it covers the authentication
plane and the Microsoft Graph data

71
00:04:48.697 --> 00:04:51.772
plane, not the SharePoint
or Exchange data planes,

72
00:04:51.772 --> 00:04:54.397
which only device-side signaling reaches.

73
00:04:54.397 --> 00:04:59.459
The setting is tenant-wide, and the pages
the guide read show no report-only mode.

74
00:04:59.459 --> 00:05:03.135
So its pilot is the Microsoft
traffic profile's assignment,

75
00:05:03.135 --> 00:05:05.085
with the block query read daily.

76
00:05:05.085 --> 00:05:09.040
The rollback turns tagging
off; the policy stays.

77
00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:14.877
How do you know tenant restrictions work?

78
00:05:14.877 --> 00:05:19.165
Learn's four-case test, run from
a pilot device with the client on.

79
00:05:19.165 --> 00:05:22.615
A member of the agency's own
tenant signs in normally.

80
00:05:22.615 --> 00:05:25.515
An account from an allowed
partner tenant signs in.

81
00:05:25.515 --> 00:05:28.651
An account from a tenant that
isn't allowed is blocked,

82
00:05:28.651 --> 00:05:32.927
and Learn's block query finds it by
error five million two hundred eleven.

83
00:05:32.927 --> 00:05:36.303
And a guest in the agency's
tenant isn't evaluated.

84
00:05:36.303 --> 00:05:37.877
Record each outcome.

85
00:05:37.877 --> 00:05:39.477
Now, where are agencies?

86
00:05:39.477 --> 00:05:44.978
Both features come with Entra I D P one or
P two and the Microsoft traffic profile,

87
00:05:44.978 --> 00:05:46.728
are generally available,

88
00:05:46.728 --> 00:05:50.728
and are available in G C C by
Learn's product-wide statement.

89
00:05:50.728 --> 00:05:54.017
Where the Microsoft traffic
profile isn't adopted yet,

90
00:05:54.017 --> 00:05:57.599
the interim path is named-location
Conditional Access and

91
00:05:57.599 --> 00:06:01.690
device-side tenant restrictions
version two by Group Policy.

92
00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:07.978
Here are the two
How-sections side by side.

93
00:06:07.978 --> 00:06:13.181
The audit phase: the compliant network
policy runs in report-only for ten business

94
00:06:13.181 --> 00:06:18.155
days, reading the would-be blocks; tenant
restrictions have no report-only mode,

95
00:06:18.155 --> 00:06:22.440
so the pilot group is the audit phase,
with the block query read daily.

96
00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:27.419
Enforcement: the compliant network policy's
state goes to enabled for the pilot,

97
00:06:27.419 --> 00:06:29.079
then ring groups are added;

98
00:06:29.079 --> 00:06:33.002
tenant restrictions widen with
the Microsoft profile's rings.

99
00:06:33.002 --> 00:06:38.049
Rollback: the policy goes back to
report-only, and in an outage the break-glass

100
00:06:38.049 --> 00:06:43.415
script does it; for tenant restrictions,
tagging is disabled and the policy stays.

101
00:06:43.415 --> 00:06:48.376
Exclusions: the emergency access group,
tested each quarter, and the Intune apps,

102
00:06:48.376 --> 00:06:52.811
with service accounts handled by
workload identity policies instead;

103
00:06:52.811 --> 00:06:56.043
tenant restrictions have
none at the tagging layer,

104
00:06:56.043 --> 00:07:00.252
and partner allowances are policies
with an owner and a review date.

105
00:07:00.252 --> 00:07:05.249
Telemetry: the sign-in query and the token
or device inconsistency alert, and for

106
00:07:05.249 --> 00:07:08.051
tenant restrictions
the block query and the

107
00:07:08.051 --> 00:07:10.777
increased external tenant activity alert.

108
00:07:10.777 --> 00:07:15.414
On day two, the security monitor watches
compliant network blocks that didn't come

109
00:07:15.414 --> 00:07:19.096
through the service, which are
either an attack or a broken client,

110
00:07:19.096 --> 00:07:23.140
and the identity engineer reviews
the partner list each quarter.

111
00:07:26.890 --> 00:07:31.715
The lab runs in a test tenant, never
production, and the authors haven't run it.

112
00:07:31.715 --> 00:07:36.555
You need Module 2's lab done, the emergency
access group, a tenant restrictions

113
00:07:36.555 --> 00:07:40.790
policy with one allowed partner,
and test accounts in three tenants.

114
00:07:40.790 --> 00:07:45.365
Step one confirms signaling and reads
the compliant network named location.

115
00:07:45.365 --> 00:07:49.040
Step two finds the two Intune
applications to exclude.

116
00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:54.553
Step three creates the pilot policy in
report-only, with the guide's exact body.

117
00:07:54.553 --> 00:07:58.052
Step four reads the would-be
blocks in the workspace.

118
00:07:58.052 --> 00:08:02.281
The guide reads them for ten business
days; a report-only failure from a

119
00:08:02.281 --> 00:08:06.802
sign-in that came through the service
is a defect to fix before enforcement.

120
00:08:06.802 --> 00:08:10.914
In a test tenant the lab shortens
that; in production, never.

121
00:08:10.914 --> 00:08:12.965
Step five proves the block.

122
00:08:12.965 --> 00:08:17.858
Confirm your emergency access account
isn't in the pilot, set the policy on, and

123
00:08:17.858 --> 00:08:22.903
on the pilot device disable the client and
sign in again in a new browser session.

124
00:08:22.903 --> 00:08:28.100
Expect "You can't access this right now."
Then put the policy back in report-only.

125
00:08:31.850 --> 00:08:36.058
Step six reads back the tenant
restrictions version two policy,

126
00:08:36.058 --> 00:08:39.075
default and partners,
and writes both to files.

127
00:08:39.075 --> 00:08:40.988
Step seven turns on tagging.

128
00:08:40.988 --> 00:08:44.806
Remember it's tenant-wide: it
acts on everyone the Microsoft

129
00:08:44.806 --> 00:08:48.701
traffic profile carries, which
in this lab is the pilot group.

130
00:08:48.701 --> 00:08:52.650
The GET should show network
packet tagging status enabled.

131
00:08:52.650 --> 00:08:57.782
Step eight runs Learn's four-case test
from the pilot device with the client on:

132
00:08:57.782 --> 00:09:02.113
a member, an allowed partner, a tenant
that isn't allowed, and a guest.

133
00:09:02.113 --> 00:09:03.687
Record each outcome.

134
00:09:03.687 --> 00:09:08.306
Step nine reads the blocks in the
workspace, with Learn's query on error

135
00:09:08.306 --> 00:09:12.687
five million two hundred eleven;
case three's sign-in appears there.

136
00:09:12.687 --> 00:09:16.938
Keep the compliant network policy
in report-only and tagging on.

137
00:09:16.938 --> 00:09:20.580
Module 6 rolls them back,
in the guide's order.

138
00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:26.941
The federal requirement, from NIST special

139
00:09:26.941 --> 00:09:30.205
publication eight hundred
fifty-three, revision five.

140
00:09:30.205 --> 00:09:35.889
The compliant network check supports A C
three, A C twelve and S C twenty-three:

141
00:09:35.889 --> 00:09:39.318
sign-in and token use
bound to the agency's path.

142
00:09:39.318 --> 00:09:44.465
The evidence is the policy export, the
named location, the report-only results,

143
00:09:44.465 --> 00:09:48.729
the disable-and-retry test, and
the break-glass rehearsal record.

144
00:09:48.729 --> 00:09:54.043
Universal tenant restrictions support
A C twenty, its enhancement one,

145
00:09:54.043 --> 00:09:58.455
and A C four: agency devices sign
in only to approved tenants.

146
00:09:58.455 --> 00:10:01.569
The evidence is the tenant
restrictions exports,

147
00:10:01.569 --> 00:10:04.380
the tagging GET, and the
four-case test record.

148
00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:10.010
Each row lines up with fed-ramp twenty X
indicators, and the program's mapping lines

149
00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:15.392
Step 7 up with O M B memo M twenty-two oh
nine's identity and network verification.

150
00:10:15.392 --> 00:10:19.560
The evidence supports the
controls; the assessor decides.

151
00:10:23.310 --> 00:10:25.285
Check yourself with two questions.

152
00:10:25.285 --> 00:10:30.035
First: in a Global Secure Access
outage, what keeps users working?

153
00:10:30.035 --> 00:10:33.970
The answer is b: Microsoft's
break-glass scripts that switch the

154
00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:38.510
policies to report-only, rehearsed
and recorded in the contingency plan.

155
00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:42.310
Turning signaling off first
would block users, not save them.

156
00:10:42.310 --> 00:10:46.560
Second: which data planes do
universal tenant restrictions reach?

157
00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:49.723
The answer is b: the
authentication plane and the

158
00:10:49.723 --> 00:10:53.360
Microsoft Graph data plane,
not SharePoint or Exchange.

159
00:10:53.360 --> 00:10:58.620
The module page has three more questions,
each linked to the unit that answers it.

160
00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:03.195
To sum up.

161
00:11:03.195 --> 00:11:06.366
You created the compliant
network policy in report-only,

162
00:11:06.366 --> 00:11:09.403
read what it would have blocked,
proved that it blocks a

163
00:11:09.403 --> 00:11:12.507
sign-in without the client,
and put it back in report-only.

164
00:11:12.507 --> 00:11:17.308
Then you turned on universal tenant
restrictions and tested all four cases.

165
00:11:17.308 --> 00:11:20.237
The evidence an I S S O
keeps from this module:

166
00:11:20.237 --> 00:11:24.630
the policy export and the named
location, the report-only results,

167
00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:29.593
the disable-and-retry record, the
break-glass rehearsal record, the tenant

168
00:11:29.593 --> 00:11:32.197
restrictions exports, the tagging GET,

169
00:11:32.197 --> 00:11:35.370
the four-case record, and
the block query result.

170
00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:39.843
Next is Module 4, private applications
and administrative paths,

171
00:11:39.843 --> 00:11:44.940
where each application gets its own path
instead of the V P N's address pool.
